00:00.280 --> 00:02.004
In this lesson, you're going to be

00:02.004 --> 00:03.730
prompting an LLM to give you back

00:03.730 --> 00:05.918
a structured response in JSON format.

00:05.918 --> 00:08.939
And then you'll use a Pydantic data model

00:08.939 --> 00:12.830
to validate whether or not the JSON contains exactly what you need.

00:13.339 --> 00:15.424
And what you'll see is that you

00:15.424 --> 00:17.510
can often get pretty close by just

00:17.510 --> 00:20.239
asking the LLM to give you the structure that you want.

00:20.609 --> 00:22.149
And then what you can do

00:22.149 --> 00:23.949
with Pydantic is catch any validation errors

00:23.949 --> 00:26.230
and pass them back to the LLM.

00:26.230 --> 00:29.370
And with this, you're creating a feedback loop so that even if

00:29.370 --> 00:33.161
the initial response from the LLM has issues that are causing errors.

00:33.161 --> 00:36.011
You can ultimately get the LLM to correct its mistakes

00:36.011 --> 00:37.701
and give you exactly what you need.

00:37.701 --> 00:39.404
And so this is sort of what you

00:39.404 --> 00:41.320
might call a traditional approach for prompting for structure.

00:41.320 --> 00:44.381
You set up a retry feedback loop

00:44.381 --> 00:47.430
and kind of assume that you're going to get there eventually,

00:47.430 --> 00:50.261
even if the LLM didn't get it right the first time.

00:50.261 --> 00:52.320
Like I said before, we're going to look at

00:52.320 --> 00:54.581
some more elegant methods after this.

00:54.581 --> 00:58.661
But it's interesting to build this feedback mechanism once yourself, so that

00:58.841 --> 01:00.365
you can get a sense for

01:00.365 --> 01:02.142
what some of those more elegant methods

01:02.142 --> 01:04.242
are handling automatically for you behind the scenes.

01:04.441 --> 01:06.242
So, let's jump into the code.

01:06.941 --> 01:09.042
Okay, so starting off with some imports,

01:09.042 --> 01:10.841
mostly stuff you've seen from the previous lesson,

01:10.841 --> 01:14.341
and you've got a couple extras here from typing,

01:14.341 --> 01:16.542
List and Literal you're going to use

01:16.542 --> 01:18.542
in the definition of a new pydantic data model.

01:19.042 --> 01:21.542
And then you're adding load_dotenv and openai

01:21.542 --> 01:23.141
to set up an openai client.

01:23.542 --> 01:26.841
Next, you can go ahead and initialize your OpenAI client.

01:28.403 --> 01:31.302
then you can set up some sample user input data.

01:31.763 --> 01:35.052
So here you have a user_input_json

01:35.052 --> 01:38.852
that contains all the fields in your user input model.

01:39.162 --> 01:42.653
And just note here that you're defining order_id and purchase_date as null

01:42.653 --> 01:44.748
in the JSON form here and those will be

01:44.748 --> 01:46.843
read in as the defaults None in your model.

01:48.322 --> 01:51.022
And then you can define the same UserInput model

01:51.022 --> 01:53.470
that you had from the previous lesson.

01:53.470 --> 01:55.918
And then just like you did at

01:55.918 --> 01:57.483
the end of the previous lesson, you

01:57.483 --> 02:01.364
can create an instance of your UserInput model using the model_validate_json

02:01.364 --> 02:04.364
method on that user_input_json.

02:05.974 --> 02:09.503
Next, you're going to define a new Pydantic model called CustomerQuery.

02:09.503 --> 02:12.283
So now this is a class called CustomerQuery

02:12.283 --> 02:15.554
and in this case, you're inheriting from UserInput.

02:15.554 --> 02:19.294
Just like any other Python class, when you inherit from another class,

02:19.294 --> 02:20.834
you get all the attributes of that

02:20.834 --> 02:22.593
class and whatever you add on to it.

02:22.593 --> 02:24.633
So this is going to be

02:24.633 --> 02:27.014
a new Pydantic data model that gets

02:27.014 --> 02:28.824
all of these fields

02:28.824 --> 02:31.764
from UserInput and you're adding on four additional fields of priority,

02:31.764 --> 02:34.405
category, is_complaint, and tags.

02:34.805 --> 02:36.555
And here priority is a string,

02:36.555 --> 02:38.460
and you have a description of what

02:38.460 --> 02:40.365
this is, priority level low, medium, high.

02:40.764 --> 02:43.814
Then category is a literal type which means it has to be

02:43.814 --> 02:47.525
one of these three options, refund_request, information_request, or other.

02:47.865 --> 02:49.414
and then is_complaint is just a Boolean

02:49.414 --> 02:50.965
that's going to be true or false.

02:50.965 --> 02:52.754
and tags is a list of strings.

02:53.115 --> 02:56.185
So let's define that. And the next thing you're going to

02:56.390 --> 02:59.431
do is construct a prompt. So first,

03:00.371 --> 03:02.670
you'll define an example response structure.

03:02.670 --> 03:05.401
So this is giving an example that you'll put in the prompt

03:05.401 --> 03:07.346
to let the LLM know the structure

03:07.346 --> 03:09.290
that you're hoping for in the response.

03:10.768 --> 03:12.937
Then, you can construct a prompt

03:12.937 --> 03:16.207
where you're saying, please analyze this user query

03:16.207 --> 03:20.307
and you're passing in this validated instance of your user input model,

03:20.307 --> 03:22.578
the JSON representation of what's in the model.

03:22.768 --> 03:25.377
And you're saying return your analysis as a JSON object

03:25.597 --> 03:27.796
matching this exact structure and data types,

03:27.796 --> 03:31.697
and then passing this example structure as the example in the prompt.

03:31.697 --> 03:33.697
And then respond only with valid JSON,

03:33.697 --> 03:36.046
do not include any other explanations or other

03:36.046 --> 03:38.396
text formatting before or after the JSON object.

03:38.396 --> 03:39.747
and then we'll just print that out.

03:39.747 --> 03:41.097
So let's go ahead and run that.

03:41.887 --> 03:43.597
So you constructed a prompt that has

03:43.597 --> 03:45.796
this format. Please analyze this user query.

03:45.796 --> 03:48.097
Here's the user input that came in.

03:48.097 --> 03:50.097
Return your analysis as a JSON object

03:50.097 --> 03:52.197
matching this structure and here's the example.

03:52.197 --> 03:54.223
and then respond only with valid

03:54.223 --> 03:56.587
JSON. you can then define a function

03:56.587 --> 03:58.957
to call the LLM.

03:58.957 --> 04:01.818
In this case setting the model as gpt-4o,

04:02.337 --> 04:04.117
then passing in your prompt

04:04.117 --> 04:07.298
and returning the content of the response.

04:08.487 --> 04:10.027
And then you can try it out.

04:12.548 --> 04:14.258
So here we called the LLM

04:14.258 --> 04:16.117
and got back this response,

04:16.117 --> 04:17.858
which looks like a

04:17.858 --> 04:20.827
fairly close to accurate response, although

04:20.827 --> 04:22.576
what you can note is that

04:22.576 --> 04:25.488
we do have some extra formatting outside. of the JSON string here

04:25.488 --> 04:27.658
and that's going to be an issue.

04:28.478 --> 04:31.307
The next thing to do is to go ahead and try

04:31.307 --> 04:33.209
and validate that response you got by

04:33.209 --> 04:36.000
attempting to create an instance of

04:36.000 --> 04:39.259
your CustomerQuery model using the model_validate_json method

04:39.259 --> 04:42.199
on that response_content. So let's see what happens when you run that.

04:43.918 --> 04:47.331
Okay, a validation error. and down here you can see that

04:47.331 --> 04:49.598
it's a validation error for CustomerQuery, invalid JSON,

04:50.019 --> 04:53.709
expected, blah, blah, blah, and you can see that it's identified

04:53.709 --> 04:57.100
what the problem is in the response format.

04:57.699 --> 04:59.629
And then just like you did in the last lesson,

05:00.019 --> 05:01.620
you're going to define a function to

05:03.480 --> 05:05.954
capture errors a little more gracefully than just having this

05:05.954 --> 05:08.430
validation error get spewed out onto the screen like this.

05:08.680 --> 05:11.499
And so you'll pass in the data_model that you want

05:11.499 --> 05:14.600
to use for validation, the llm_response that you want to validate.

05:14.759 --> 05:19.939
You'll try validating that response with the model_validate_json method of your model.

05:20.250 --> 05:22.990
And if that works, great. Otherwise, you'll capture that validation

05:23.081 --> 05:25.290
error and and print it out.

05:25.290 --> 05:28.831
And what you're going to return is either the validated_data, and None,

05:28.831 --> 05:31.170
or None and the error_message.

05:31.170 --> 05:33.230
So it's basically a tuple that you're returning

05:33.230 --> 05:36.990
that either contains the valid data and nothing for the error,

05:36.990 --> 05:39.230
or nothing for the valid data and the

05:39.230 --> 05:41.471
error. And then you can try that out.

05:41.471 --> 05:47.031
So here you captured just the tail end of that validation error,

05:47.031 --> 05:50.040
explaining what went wrong and the issue with the JSON.

05:50.040 --> 05:52.310
And then what you're going to do with this

05:52.552 --> 05:55.211
error that you've captured is to construct a new prompt

05:55.211 --> 05:57.096
to send this back to the LLM

05:57.096 --> 05:58.982
and ask it to fix its mistake.

05:59.822 --> 06:02.221
And to do that, you're going to define a new function.

06:02.221 --> 06:04.351
This function is called create_retry_prompt

06:04.351 --> 06:06.012
where you're passing in the original_prompt,

06:06.012 --> 06:09.651
the original_response from the LLM and the error_message you got back.

06:10.002 --> 06:13.141
And then your retry_prompt that you're constructing is a message that says,

06:13.141 --> 06:14.761
this is a request to fix an

06:14.761 --> 06:17.911
error in the structure of an LLM response. Here's the original prompt.

06:17.911 --> 06:20.012
Here's the original LLM response,

06:20.012 --> 06:21.822
and here's the error message that

06:21.822 --> 06:24.023
was generated when trying to parse this into the model.

06:24.023 --> 06:25.853
Compare the error message and the llm_response and

06:25.853 --> 06:27.682
identify what needs to be fixed or removed

06:27.682 --> 06:29.673
in the llm_response to resolve this error.

06:29.673 --> 06:31.642
And again, respond ONLY with valid JSON.

06:31.642 --> 06:33.163
Do not include any explanations or other text

06:33.163 --> 06:34.682
or formatting before or after the JSON string.

06:34.682 --> 06:36.598
And then you're returning this as something that

06:36.598 --> 06:38.512
you can use in a new LLM call.

06:38.512 --> 06:40.612
So let's run that to define the function.

06:40.612 --> 06:42.668
And then you can try running this

06:42.668 --> 06:44.723
function to construct a validation retry prompt

06:44.723 --> 06:46.963
and print it out.

06:46.963 --> 06:49.103
So when you do that, you can

06:49.103 --> 06:51.244
see what you're going to send off.

06:51.244 --> 06:53.903
now to the LLM as a follow-up request.

06:53.903 --> 06:55.588
and it says, here's a request to

06:55.588 --> 06:57.274
fix an error. Here's the original prompt,

06:57.274 --> 07:01.134
and so this includes the entire original prompt down to here.

07:01.494 --> 07:03.024
And then here's the original response,

07:03.524 --> 07:06.514
just to show the LLM what was returned the first time.

07:07.434 --> 07:09.805
and then this generated the error. This is your validation

07:09.805 --> 07:12.413
error that came back from attempting to populate your pydantic model.

07:12.413 --> 07:14.533
and then try to go ahead and fix this.

07:14.533 --> 07:16.353
So that's the prompt we're going to send off now.

07:17.233 --> 07:20.103
So you can go ahead and call the LLM again.

07:21.214 --> 07:22.668
Now passing in the validation retry

07:22.668 --> 07:24.365
prompt and see what you get back.

07:25.514 --> 07:27.935
Okay, cool. Looks like it cleaned it up.

07:27.935 --> 07:30.845
Now you've got none of that markdown formatting

07:30.845 --> 07:33.754
outside and what looks to be valid JSON.

07:34.185 --> 07:38.444
So next, you can go ahead and attempt to validate with model,

07:38.444 --> 07:40.735
that new response that you've got, the

07:40.735 --> 07:43.024
validation retry response, and see what happens.

07:43.464 --> 07:47.254
Huh, another validation error. Okay. But this is different.

07:47.774 --> 07:49.326
So now it says one validation

07:49.326 --> 07:51.136
error for CustomerQuery query, input should be

07:51.136 --> 07:55.636
uh in category, input should be refund_request, information_request or other.

07:55.935 --> 07:59.735
So up here, this is a category of account_issue, which,

07:59.935 --> 08:03.836
if you recall back up here in the definition of your model,

08:04.636 --> 08:06.735
you have category defined as a literal,

08:07.435 --> 08:10.935
which can only take the values of refund_request, information_request or other.

08:11.435 --> 08:13.836
And now what you've done in the

08:13.836 --> 08:16.235
example_response_structure is provide one of those, but

08:16.735 --> 08:17.889
this is a problem arising from

08:17.889 --> 08:19.235
the fact that the LLM doesn't know

08:19.476 --> 08:20.451
what the other ones are. So this

08:20.451 --> 08:21.427
was not quite set up for success,

08:21.427 --> 08:22.382
but let's go ahead and see

08:22.382 --> 08:23.497
what happens if you capture this error

08:23.497 --> 08:26.197
and create another retry prompt.

08:26.197 --> 08:28.067
So you can use

08:28.067 --> 08:30.050
your create retry prompt function again

08:30.050 --> 08:32.366
to create a second validation retry prompt.

08:32.366 --> 08:34.956
And now you're passing in

08:34.956 --> 08:37.366
the validation_retry_prompt

08:37.366 --> 08:39.572
as the original_prompt and the validation

08:39.572 --> 08:42.147
retry and the validation_retry_response as the original_response

08:42.147 --> 08:44.197
and the validation_error.

08:44.197 --> 08:46.287
So you can run that to see what you get.

08:46.287 --> 08:48.017
And now this is getting to be a bit

08:48.677 --> 08:50.388
bit of a cumbersome prompt,

08:50.388 --> 08:52.638
but you can see this is a request to fix an error

08:52.638 --> 08:54.041
and then the original prompt was

08:54.041 --> 08:55.677
also a request to fix an error

08:56.227 --> 08:59.628
and so you can read through the entire original retry prompt

08:59.628 --> 09:04.498
and eventually you'll get down to where you had a new llm_response

09:04.498 --> 09:07.577
and this generated a new validation error,

09:07.577 --> 09:09.628
and now we need to fix that.

09:09.628 --> 09:11.312
So, you have a new prompt that

09:11.312 --> 09:13.043
you can send off to the LLM

09:13.043 --> 09:15.023
and you can use that to call the

09:15.023 --> 09:16.587
LLM again now with the second validation retry

09:18.129 --> 09:22.578
retry prompt and see what you get back. Okay, well,

09:22.989 --> 09:25.999
so now it's a valid category, information_request,

09:26.249 --> 09:29.828
but unfortunately, you got back this JSON markdown formatting again.

09:30.068 --> 09:31.940
So the purpose of this lesson is

09:31.940 --> 09:34.078
in some sense to have you experience firsthand

09:34.078 --> 09:36.092
the challenge of getting back a structured response

09:36.092 --> 09:38.359
in exactly the format you want from an LLM,

09:38.359 --> 09:41.999
and just some of the common issues that arise from that.

09:42.639 --> 09:44.589
And what you'll do next

09:44.589 --> 09:47.178
is to set up a complete retry cycle.

09:47.419 --> 09:50.250
system so that you can send an initial prompt

09:50.250 --> 09:51.879
If you have a problem with the

09:51.879 --> 09:53.509
JSON or with populating your data model,

09:53.509 --> 09:55.259
capture that validation error,

09:55.259 --> 09:58.679
construct a retry prompt and do it again and

09:58.679 --> 10:00.529
give yourself some number of retries.

10:00.529 --> 10:03.100
In this case, we're setting the default number of retries to five,

10:03.100 --> 10:04.606
to see if you can get to

10:04.606 --> 10:06.330
a valid response after some number of retries.

10:06.330 --> 10:09.429
So here you're defining a function called validate_llm_response

10:09.429 --> 10:11.635
passing in a prompt, a data_model,

10:11.635 --> 10:14.210
number of retries and a LLM model.

10:14.690 --> 10:16.419
setting up an initial LLM call

10:16.791 --> 10:19.291
to get back a response.

10:19.291 --> 10:23.091
And then for as many attempts as you're giving yourself in retries,

10:23.091 --> 10:26.270
you'll try first just validating the LLM response you got.

10:26.270 --> 10:28.259
If there's a problem with that,

10:28.259 --> 10:30.581
then first just print out some information.

10:30.770 --> 10:34.640
and then construct a validation retry prompt and try it again.

10:34.640 --> 10:37.850
call the LLM. If you get back valid data, that's great.

10:37.850 --> 10:41.139
If not, you'll run this loop as many times as you either

10:41.139 --> 10:42.783
need to to get valid data

10:42.783 --> 10:44.701
or until you run out of retries.

10:45.061 --> 10:46.663
So you can go ahead and

10:46.663 --> 10:48.532
define that. and then try it out.

10:48.532 --> 10:50.595
So here you're just starting with

10:50.595 --> 10:53.001
the same original prompt that you constructed

10:53.001 --> 10:55.369
at the top of the lesson

10:55.369 --> 10:58.131
and passing in your CustomerQuery data model.

10:59.221 --> 11:02.192
And when you run that, you see you've got

11:02.192 --> 11:04.527
one validation error to do with

11:04.527 --> 11:07.251
CustomerQuery, running into JSON problems like before.

11:07.251 --> 11:11.062
And so retry zero or what was the initial attempt failed,

11:11.062 --> 11:13.287
and you're trying again. And then it turns

11:13.287 --> 11:15.511
out on the first retry, you got more

11:15.533 --> 11:17.653
invalid JSON, the problem was not corrected

11:17.653 --> 11:19.283
and so that retry failed.

11:19.283 --> 11:22.182
And then eventually you get to the issue with category

11:22.182 --> 11:25.003
that it should be refund_request, information_request or other,

11:25.003 --> 11:27.072
and so retry 2 of 5 failed,

11:27.072 --> 11:28.857
but then in running a third retry,

11:28.857 --> 11:30.643
you actually got to an LLM response

11:30.643 --> 11:33.143
that was able to be validated in your model.

11:33.682 --> 11:35.773
And what you'll see if you run this multiple times

11:35.773 --> 11:37.523
is that it's a little different every time,

11:37.523 --> 11:41.322
because LLMs never quite do the same thing every time

11:41.322 --> 11:43.503
and you never know quite what to expect.

11:43.503 --> 11:47.104
So here again, you've got got a JSON issue, a JSON issue,

11:47.504 --> 11:51.504
then uh validation error with the category field, then another JSON issue,

11:52.303 --> 11:54.604
and then finally you've got to valid data.

11:55.104 --> 11:59.004
So sometimes it takes you three failed attempts, sometimes two.

11:59.604 --> 12:01.703
If you run this enough times, you might go through

12:01.703 --> 12:03.803
all five attempts and actually never get to valid data.

12:03.803 --> 12:06.004
And so you might be thinking at this point

12:06.004 --> 12:07.388
that this looks like kind of

12:07.388 --> 12:09.004
a flaky system. And you'd be right.

12:09.403 --> 12:11.794
The fact of the matter is, I've kind of set this

12:11.794 --> 12:13.845
up in such a way that you're bound

12:13.845 --> 12:15.894
to experience these errors. errors and retry requests,

12:15.894 --> 12:19.184
and there are certainly ways to construct your initial prompts to

12:19.184 --> 12:22.434
set yourself up for a better likelihood of success.

12:22.434 --> 12:25.475
But what you're experiencing here is actually what's going

12:25.475 --> 12:28.855
on behind the scenes when you're using a retry library

12:28.855 --> 12:31.184
that handles all this on the back end for you.

12:31.184 --> 12:33.274
And in the next lesson, you'll try that out.

12:33.274 --> 12:36.024
But I wanted to give you the first-hand experience here of

12:36.024 --> 12:37.898
seeing what's happening when you're asking

12:37.898 --> 12:40.085
for a structured response from an LLM,

12:40.085 --> 12:41.845
getting something that doesn't quite match,

12:41.845 --> 12:45.125
and going back and retrying. by passing back the error messages.

12:45.405 --> 12:46.966
This is exactly how this situation is handled

12:46.966 --> 12:48.525
in the back-end logic of some of these

12:49.566 --> 12:51.233
libraries that'll do it for you. So

12:51.233 --> 12:52.900
before we go on to the next

12:52.900 --> 12:54.605
lesson, I just want to show you one more

12:54.605 --> 12:56.571
trick you can use with pydantic models

12:56.571 --> 12:58.535
that'll help you construct a better prompt

12:58.535 --> 13:00.586
initially, if you want to set up a

13:00.586 --> 13:02.635
retry and feedback loop like we just did.

13:03.025 --> 13:05.725
So here what you're going to do is to print out the

13:06.485 --> 13:09.905
model JSON schema of your CustomerQuery data model.

13:10.186 --> 13:11.885
And when you do that, what you

13:11.885 --> 13:13.826
see is that even this relatively simple model

13:13.826 --> 13:16.797
has a complicated looking JSON schema.

13:16.797 --> 13:18.351
And while this is a little

13:18.351 --> 13:20.167
bit difficult for a human to parse,

13:20.167 --> 13:23.547
this is exactly the thing that will let an LLM know

13:23.547 --> 13:26.927
much more clearly what you're looking for in your structured response.

13:26.927 --> 13:29.392
So, rather than providing an example, like we did

13:29.392 --> 13:31.856
in the prompt at the top of the lesson,

13:31.856 --> 13:35.956
if you instead provide the model schema of your pydantic data model,

13:35.956 --> 13:37.850
you'll often get to the structured response

13:37.850 --> 13:40.017
you need from the LLM much more quickly.

13:40.017 --> 13:41.537
So, as a reminder,

13:41.537 --> 13:43.837
the prompt that we started with

13:43.837 --> 13:46.118
is one that looks like this.

13:46.118 --> 13:48.518
where you pass in the user input

13:48.518 --> 13:50.035
and then gave an example of

13:50.035 --> 13:51.807
the kind of output you're looking for.

13:51.807 --> 13:52.980
And this is really common, a

13:52.980 --> 13:54.347
lot of people use this approach for

13:54.347 --> 13:56.717
whatever kind of output you're trying to get from the LLM,

13:56.717 --> 13:58.702
providing examples in the prompt can be a

13:58.702 --> 14:00.687
way to get to the response you want.

14:00.687 --> 14:04.427
However, a better starting point would actually be

14:04.427 --> 14:06.337
providing a prompt like this

14:06.337 --> 14:09.238
where you pass in the user_input as before,

14:09.238 --> 14:10.759
but instead of that example, pass in

14:10.759 --> 14:12.499
your data_model_schema. schema that you defined up here,

14:12.499 --> 14:15.548
this long JSON schema

14:15.548 --> 14:18.448
that describes exactly what your model expects.

14:18.698 --> 14:20.828
When you create a prompt like that,

14:20.828 --> 14:23.433
you're giving better instructions to the LLM as

14:23.433 --> 14:26.038
to what you're hoping for in the response.

14:26.528 --> 14:29.989
And you can try out that new prompt in your validate_llm_response

14:29.989 --> 14:33.558
retry function and see what you get.

14:35.969 --> 14:39.519
So in this case, you had one issue still with Invalid JSON,

14:39.519 --> 14:41.574
but after that, a single re retry

14:41.574 --> 14:43.629
allowed you to get to valid data.

14:44.629 --> 14:47.330
And this is another example of what's happening behind the scenes when,

14:47.330 --> 14:50.029
as you'll see in the next lesson, you can pass your

14:50.429 --> 14:52.230
pydantic data model in your API

14:52.230 --> 14:54.330
call and what's often happening is the

14:54.629 --> 14:56.981
data_model_schema is being extracted from your model and

14:56.981 --> 14:59.629
used as part of the prompt to the LLM.

15:00.129 --> 15:01.422
And with that, you have a

15:01.422 --> 15:02.929
new set of tools in your toolkit

15:03.330 --> 15:05.929
for getting the structure that you want from an LLM.

15:06.629 --> 15:08.629
Even when it doesn't get it right the first time.

15:09.029 --> 15:10.700
It turns out that this exact mechanism

15:10.700 --> 15:12.370
mechanism is at work behind the scenes

15:12.370 --> 15:13.800
in some of the frameworks that allow

15:13.800 --> 15:15.230
you to pass your pydantic data model

15:15.230 --> 15:18.020
in directly in your API call.

15:18.020 --> 15:22.451
Namely, that mechanism of extracting the JSON schema from your pydantic model,

15:22.451 --> 15:24.571
using that to construct a prompt,

15:24.921 --> 15:28.390
and then handling validation and retrying in a feedback loop.

15:29.240 --> 15:30.747
And that's where we're going next.

15:30.747 --> 15:32.504
In the next lesson, we'll look at

15:32.504 --> 15:34.270
a variety of frameworks for getting the structure

15:34.270 --> 15:36.951
you want by passing your pydantic model directly

15:36.951 --> 15:39.630
in your API call. I'll see you there.